    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Elastic dislocation homogeneous medium are commonly used to model crustal deformation processes. However. the Earth's crust is obviously inhomogeneous, and material inhomogeneities especially layered inhomogeneities play crucial rotes in many crustal processes such as faulting and jointing. In this paper, we apply the module perturbation method of Du et al. (1994) to study the effect of layered inhomogeneity on deformation and stress field around a dip-slip fault. In the perturbation procedure, a homogeneous medium is chosen to obtain the zeroth-order solution. For the medium with only piecewise constant variations in shear modulous, the first-order correction to the displacements is given by a surface integral (line integral in 2-D) involving the spatial variations in shear modulous, the displacements in a homogeneous medium, and the Green's function for the homogenous medium. It is found that a soft surface layer amplifies but a stiff surface layer reduces the peak surface displacements respectively. A soft surface layer increases the elastic strain energy stored in the system and thereby enhance fault propagation. A stiff surface layer, on the other hand, decreases the elastic strain energy and thereby impede fault propagation. These results can help us to better understand the physical continuity of faults and joints in layered sedimentary rocks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
